<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Second pregnancy at 9 weeks by a certain last menstrual period, with the uterus about 12 to 13 weeks in size and heavier nausea than in her first pregnancy. Please perform a first-trimester ultrasound at 11 weeks to establish the dates and the cause of the large uterus, and advise on her prenatal care. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks; sooner for bleeding, pain, or severe vomiting. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What explains the large uterus: a dating error, a multiple pregnancy, fibroids, or a molar pregnancy?</item><item>If there is more than one fetus, what is the chorionicity, and what surveillance will she need?</item><item>Which aneuploidy screening options suit her pregnancy?</item><item>Does she need low-dose aspirin?</item><item>How should her iron deficiency anemia be managed?</item><item>What should be done to assess and lower the risk of preterm birth?</item><item>When and where should she plan to give birth?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Cleopatra is seen at the request of Dr. Arvanitis-Osei at 11 weeks 0 days. Her first pregnancy, seven years ago, went to term without complications, by her account, with a vaginal birth. She has regular cycles and a certain last period. Nausea has eased on doxylamine with pyridoxine. No bleeding or pain. No family history of twins that she knows of and no fertility treatment. She leads a demanding business and wants to keep working; she prefers clear plans and women clinicians. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Cleopatra VII Philopator was born in early 69 BC (some sources allow late 70 BC), daughter of Ptolemy XII Auletes; her mother is not identified with certainty. [D]</item><item>She was of Macedonian Greek descent, from the dynasty founded by Ptolemy I Soter; her first language was Koine Greek, and she is the only Ptolemaic ruler known to have learned the Egyptian language. [D]</item><item>Plutarch writes that her tongue, &apos;like an instrument of many strings&apos;, could turn to whatever language she pleased, and that she seldom needed an interpreter with Ethiopians, Troglodytes, Hebrews, Arabians, Syrians, Medes, or Parthians. [D]</item><item>She was queen of Egypt from 51 BC to 30 BC. [D]</item><item>Her first child, a son, Ptolemy Caesar, called Caesarion, was born in 47 BC, possibly on 23 June; Plutarch says he &apos;was believed to be&apos; the son of the Roman dictator. [D]</item><item>Mark Antony summoned her to Cilicia in 41 BC; she drew him to Alexandria, where he spent the winter of 41 to 40 BC with her, and he left Egypt by the spring of 40 BC. [D]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

