    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Uterus larger than dates in early pregnancy: 9 weeks by a certain last menstrual period, with the uterus about 12 to 13 weeks in size; nausea and vomiting heavier than in her first pregnancy; no bleeding; cause not yet known (multiple pregnancy, dating error, fibroids, or molar pregnancy)</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>O26.841</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting finding anchored to the documented conception in the winter of 41 to 40 BC; it resolves when the ultrasound explains it.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Nausea and vomiting of pregnancy, moderate (PUQE score 9); keeping fluids down; no weight loss or ketonuria</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>O21.9</td><td>S — Synthetic; nausea is common and often heavier in early pregnancy with a large uterus.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Iron deficiency anemia in pregnancy: hemoglobin 10.8 g/dL, MCV 79 fL, ferritin 14 ng/mL</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>O99.011</td><td>S — Synthetic finding; it echoes no documented diagnosis.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Twin pregnancy, dichorionic diamniotic, second pregnancy (gravida 2, para 1): two live fetuses with a thick dividing membrane and a twin-peak (lambda) sign at 11 weeks; dated by her last period, confirmed by the crown-rump lengths</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>O30.041</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis anchored to the documented twins of 40 BC; chorionicity is a modern finding and makes no claim about her real pregnancy.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

